<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romulus Augustulus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">deposed <a href="/years/476">476 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> Augustulus was the last emperor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>, a youth placed on the throne by his father, the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orestes-roman-general">Orestes</a>. He was deposed in 476 CE by the Germanic commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a>, a date conventionally used to mark the end of Roman rule in the West. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> note that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s coronation in 800 created the first Western emperor since his deposition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus.jpg" alt="Romulus Augustulus" width="320" height="311" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ANS · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_Solidus_of_Romulus_Augustulus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> was born between about 460 and 465 and was named after his maternal grandfather, a nobleman from Poetovio in Noricum, a comes who was sent on an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila</a> in 449. His father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orestes-roman-general">Orestes</a> was a Roman citizen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> who had once served as secretary to Attila. The name of his mother is not known; it might have been Barbaria. On his accession he took Augustus as a proper name, and his coins read Romulus Augustus. The nickname Augustulus, little Augustus, was a derisive reference to his youth, never in official use, and in Greek his first name was twisted into Momylus, little disgrace.</p><p>In 475 the emperor Julius Nepos named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orestes-roman-general">Orestes</a> magister militum and sent him against rebelling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a>. Orestes instead heeded the grievances of his troops, many of them foederati, and marched on Ravenna, which he entered on 28 August 475; Nepos escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amfissa">Salona</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After an interregnum of two months <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orestes-roman-general">Orestes</a> proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> emperor on 31 October 475, when he may have been as young as ten. Romulus was little more than a figurehead, and his father ran much of the administration. The eastern emperor Zeno never recognised him, since Nepos, invested by Leo I, still claimed the title in exile. In 476 the foederati in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, mainly Herules, Scirians and Turcilingians, demanded land to settle on. Orestes refused, and under their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> they defeated and executed him at Ticinum on 28 August 476.</p><p>On 4 September Odoacer captured Ravenna and deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a>. He became the first King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, sent the western regalia to Zeno and ruled without a further emperor in the West, nominally under Zeno's authority. Odoacer spared Romulus, on account of his youth and beauty, with a pension of 6,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidi</a> and an estate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, the castellum Lucullanum. Little is known of his later life. He may have helped to found a monastery there around the remains of Saint Severinus, and a letter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodoric-the-great">Theodoric the Great</a> of 507 or 511 to a Romulus may refer to him. Very few records of his reign survive, and no policies, laws or significant inscriptions are known.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Romulus Augustulus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He was the first emperor in the West since the deposition of <b>Romulus Augustulus</b> in 476 CE, and the coronation established the ideal of a unified Christian Europe under Carolingian leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On 4 September 476 CE the Germanic commander Odoacer deposed <b>Romulus Augustulus</b>, the last Western emperor, and sent the imperial regalia to Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>He was the first emperor in the West since the deposition of <b>Romulus Augustulus</b> in 476.</blockquote><a href="/essays/christmas-day-in-history">Christmas Day in History</a></li><li><blockquote>By the time Odoacer deposed the boy emperor <b>Romulus Augustulus</b> in 476, the western state had been shrinking for a generation into something closer to an Italian kingdom with an imperial title.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li><li><blockquote>Orestes then made his young son <b>Romulus Augustulus</b> emperor; he would be the last to rule in the West.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-28">28 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Germanic commander Odoacer deposed the boy emperor <b>Romulus Augustulus</b> and sent the imperial regalia to Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-4">4 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bosnia-and-herzegovina">Bosnia and Herzegovina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.9,19.02&amp;year=476" aria-label="Pannonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5003 578 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Romulus Augustulus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5453 848h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5453 848h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.9,19.02&amp;year=476">What was here in 476 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
